Every failure is written to the app's log, where it is reported or where the person is told
Kenneth's rule: "also make sure the log captures everything. expected, or
unexpected." Many failures reached the person, or a report, and left no
line in the app's log, so a log attached to a report could not say what had
happened.

The log is written where a failure leaves, not where it starts:

- Unexpected errors: the top handler already logged. report_if_automatic,
  used by timers, HTTP callbacks and the app-start report, now logs too.
- Expected errors carry what the other side said. AgentError and
  PrintReportAnswer have a field, said, filled where the AI service, JusPrin's
  sign-in or the print report answers: its status, request id and words. It
  is written to the log only, never sent to a page or saved.
- One line where the person is told: AgentHost::told() for every error sent
  to the AI page, HomeHost::told() for every problem sent to the Home page,
  the two JusPrin dialog functions, the error screens of the AI page, the
  Home page, the printer window and the account pages, and the tool
  server's refusals to an outside AI client.
- Failures that become part of what a screen shows, not a message, keep one
  line where that state is decided: a print host that stopped answering
  (once per change, without its API key), a printer connection that was not
  verified, an unreadable OrcaSlicer.conf or preset the import did not load,
  an email sign-in refusal, an outside tool call refused because no project
  is open, and a stale discovery file at quit (was std::cerr). An unreadable
  OrcaSlicer.conf is not thrown: the import check runs on every Home refresh
  on the first-run Printers step, so it would report again on each refresh
  and stop that screen from drawing. The discovery cleanup runs in a
  destructor, which cannot throw.
- Failures between a page and the app, both our own code, are thrown to the
  message's top handler, which reports them: the AI page's refused
  handshake, and the Home page's unreadable message, refused handshake,
  message of an unknown type and message before its handshake. The page's
  client always sends hello first, and the app never reloads Home itself.

